  {
    id:"uni_b1", name:"University — Logic & Fallacies", emoji:"🧠", cards:[
      { t:"mc", q:"An ad hominem fallacy occurs when an argument does what?", a:"Attacks the person instead of their argument", w:["Appeals to expert evidence","Defines terms clearly","Uses a valid syllogism"], e:"Ad hominem targets the individual rather than addressing the actual claim." },
      { t:"mc", q:"A straw man fallacy involves what?", a:"Misrepresenting an opponent argument to attack it more easily", w:["Citing reliable statistics","Presenting both sides fairly","Drawing a valid conclusion"], e:"The straw man distorts a position into a weaker form, then refutes that distortion." },
      { t:"mc", q:"A false dilemma fallacy presents what?", a:"Only two options when more actually exist", w:["A full range of choices","A balanced comparison","A tested hypothesis"], e:"A false dilemma (either-or fallacy) wrongly limits the choices to two extremes." },
      { t:"tf", q:"An appeal to popularity argues that a claim is true simply because many people believe it.", a:"True", e:"The bandwagon or appeal to popularity treats wide belief as proof of truth, which is fallacious." },
      { t:"type", q:"Assuming an event caused another merely because it came before it is the post hoc ______ fallacy.", a:"ergo propter hoc", accepts:["ergo propter hoc","propter hoc"], e:"Post hoc ergo propter hoc wrongly infers causation from mere sequence in time." },
      { t:"mc", q:"A slippery slope fallacy claims what?", a:"One small step will inevitably lead to extreme consequences", w:["Two premises yield a valid conclusion","Evidence supports a moderate claim","A definition is precise"], e:"The slippery slope assumes an unsupported chain reaction of dire outcomes." },
      { t:"mc", q:"An appeal to authority becomes fallacious when the authority cited is what?", a:"Not a genuine expert on the matter", w:["A peer-reviewed specialist","Directly relevant and qualified","Widely tested and reliable"], e:"Citing an irrelevant or unqualified authority does not properly support a claim." },
      { t:"mc", q:"A categorical syllogism is composed of how many statements?", a:"Two premises and a conclusion", w:["One premise only","Four premises","A premise and a question"], e:"A standard categorical syllogism has a major premise, a minor premise, and a conclusion." },
      { t:"tf", q:"In the syllogism all men are mortal, Socrates is a man, therefore Socrates is mortal, the conclusion follows validly.", a:"True", e:"The conclusion is logically entailed by the two premises, making the argument valid." },
      { t:"mc", q:"In logic, an argument is valid when what is true?", a:"If the premises are true, the conclusion must be true", w:["The premises are always factual","The conclusion is popular","The speaker is an expert"], e:"Validity concerns form: true premises guarantee a true conclusion regardless of content." },
      { t:"mc", q:"A sound argument is one that is what?", a:"Valid and has all true premises", w:["Valid but with false premises","Persuasive but invalid","Long and detailed"], e:"Soundness requires both a valid structure and actually true premises." },
      { t:"mc", q:"Deductive reasoning moves from what to what?", a:"General premises to a specific conclusion", w:["Specific cases to a general rule","Emotion to evidence","Questions to definitions"], e:"Deduction applies general principles to reach a specific, certain conclusion." },
      { t:"mc", q:"Inductive reasoning typically moves in which direction?", a:"From specific observations to a general conclusion", w:["From general law to certainty","From authority to emotion","From conclusion to premise"], e:"Induction generalises from observed instances, yielding probable rather than certain conclusions." },
      { t:"tf", q:"Begging the question is a fallacy in which the conclusion is assumed within the premises.", a:"True", e:"Begging the question is circular reasoning where the claim to be proved is taken as given." },
      { t:"mc", q:"A red herring fallacy does what to an argument?", a:"Introduces an irrelevant point to distract from the issue", w:["Strengthens the conclusion with data","Clarifies the main claim","Defines the key terms"], e:"A red herring diverts attention away from the actual question under discussion." },
      { t:"mc", q:"The first step of the scientific method is usually which of the following?", a:"Making an observation and asking a question", w:["Publishing the final results","Drawing a marketing plan","Selling the product"], e:"Scientific inquiry begins with observation and a question that leads to a hypothesis." },
      { t:"type", q:"A testable, falsifiable proposed explanation in the scientific method is called a ______.", a:"hypothesis", accepts:["Hypothesis"], e:"A hypothesis is a tentative, testable explanation that experiments can support or refute." },
      { t:"tf", q:"A scientific hypothesis must be falsifiable, meaning it can in principle be proven wrong.", a:"True", e:"Falsifiability is a key criterion distinguishing scientific claims from unfalsifiable ones." },
      { t:"mc", q:"In an experiment, the variable deliberately changed by the researcher is called the what?", a:"Independent variable", w:["Dependent variable","Control variable","Constant"], e:"The independent variable is manipulated to observe its effect on the dependent variable." },
      { t:"mc", q:"A hasty generalisation fallacy is committed when a conclusion is drawn from what?", a:"Too small or unrepresentative a sample", w:["A large random sample","A controlled experiment","A peer-reviewed study"], e:"Hasty generalisation overreaches from insufficient evidence to a broad claim." }
    ]
  },
  {
    id:"uni_b2", name:"University — Study & Research Skills", emoji:"📚", cards:[
      { t:"mc", q:"Plagiarism is best defined as what?", a:"Presenting another person work or ideas as your own", w:["Quoting a source with full citation","Writing in your own words from memory","Summarising with acknowledgement"], e:"Plagiarism is using others words or ideas without proper attribution." },
      { t:"mc", q:"Which practice helps a student avoid plagiarism?", a:"Citing sources and using quotation marks for direct quotes", w:["Copying text and changing one word","Removing all references","Submitting a friend essay"], e:"Proper citation, quotation, and paraphrasing with attribution prevent plagiarism." },
      { t:"tf", q:"Paraphrasing a source still requires you to acknowledge the original author.", a:"True", e:"Even reworded ideas must be credited, otherwise it is still plagiarism." },
      { t:"mc", q:"A citation in academic writing primarily does what?", a:"Acknowledges the source of information or ideas", w:["Increases the word count for marks","Decorates the page","Hides the author identity"], e:"Citations give credit, allow verification, and support academic integrity." },
      { t:"type", q:"An alphabetical list of all sources cited at the end of a paper is called a ______ or reference list.", a:"bibliography", accepts:["Bibliography","references"], e:"The bibliography or reference list records full details of sources used." },
      { t:"mc", q:"A topic sentence in a paragraph usually does what?", a:"States the main idea of the paragraph", w:["Lists the references","Repeats the title exactly","Ends every paragraph"], e:"The topic sentence introduces the controlling idea that the rest of the paragraph develops." },
      { t:"mc", q:"Effective paragraphing requires that each paragraph generally does what?", a:"Focuses on one main idea", w:["Covers many unrelated points","Contains only one word","Avoids any topic sentence"], e:"Unity means a paragraph develops a single main idea with supporting detail." },
      { t:"tf", q:"Coherence in writing refers to the logical flow and connection between ideas.", a:"True", e:"Coherent writing links sentences and paragraphs so the reasoning is easy to follow." },
      { t:"mc", q:"The Cornell note-taking system divides the page into which main areas?", a:"Cues, notes, and a summary", w:["Title, price, and date","Header, footer, and margin only","Question, answer, and signature"], e:"Cornell notes use a cue column, a main notes area, and a summary section for review." },
      { t:"mc", q:"Active note-taking during a lecture is more effective than what?", a:"Copying everything word for word", w:["Summarising key points in your own words","Noting questions to follow up","Organising notes under headings"], e:"Verbatim copying limits understanding; paraphrasing and structuring aid retention." },
      { t:"mc", q:"In a library, the catalogue is mainly used to do what?", a:"Locate and identify available materials", w:["Pay tuition fees","Print examination results","Reserve a sports field"], e:"The catalogue helps users find books and resources by author, title, or subject." },
      { t:"type", q:"A short academic phrase warning against copying, the principle of academic ______, demands honesty in scholarship.", a:"integrity", accepts:["Integrity"], e:"Academic integrity requires honesty, trust, and proper acknowledgement in all academic work." },
      { t:"mc", q:"A primary source in research is best described as what?", a:"An original first-hand record or data", w:["A textbook summarising others work","An encyclopedia entry","A book review"], e:"Primary sources are original materials such as data, interviews, or eyewitness accounts." },
      { t:"tf", q:"A secondary source interprets, analyses, or summarises primary sources.", a:"True", e:"Secondary sources comment on or synthesise primary materials rather than presenting original data." },
      { t:"mc", q:"Skimming a text is a reading technique used mainly to do what?", a:"Get a general overview quickly", w:["Memorise every detail","Translate each word","Edit the grammar"], e:"Skimming surveys headings and key sentences to grasp the gist rapidly." },
      { t:"mc", q:"Scanning a text is best suited for which purpose?", a:"Finding specific information such as a date or name", w:["Understanding deep themes","Enjoying a novel slowly","Checking spelling line by line"], e:"Scanning quickly locates particular facts without reading everything." },
      { t:"mc", q:"The 1999 Constitution of the Federal Republic of Nigeria is best described as what?", a:"The supreme law of the land", w:["A political party manifesto","A university handbook","An international treaty"], e:"The Constitution is supreme, and any law inconsistent with it is void to that extent." },
      { t:"mc", q:"The three arms of government recognised in the Nigerian Constitution are which of the following?", a:"The Legislature, the Executive, and the Judiciary", w:["The Army, Navy, and Air Force","The Federal, State, and Local councils only","The Senate, Cabinet, and Police"], e:"Separation of powers divides authority among the Legislature, Executive, and Judiciary." },
      { t:"tf", q:"Under the Nigerian Constitution, the National Assembly is made up of the Senate and the House of Representatives.", a:"True", e:"The bicameral National Assembly comprises the Senate and the House of Representatives." },
      { t:"type", q:"In Nigeria, the highest court and final appellate court is the ______ Court.", a:"Supreme", accepts:["supreme"], e:"The Supreme Court of Nigeria is the apex court whose decisions are final." }
    ]
  }
